\details{
Use this when the spatial data no longer comfortably fits in memory, which in
practice means whole slide images. On a 283-slide cohort of 342 million cells the
in-memory spatial slot is 34.2 GB against 2.19 GB of parquet, and since the
parent process must hold all of it while \code{mclapply()} forks, a multi-core run
cannot start at all. Disk-backing makes peak memory scale with \code{workers} rather
than with the number of samples.

It is not a general speed-up. For \code{\link[=ripleys_k]{ripleys_k()}} the per-worker pair list
overtakes the spatial frame at roughly \code{max(r_range) = 30}, so at the default
\code{r_range} the frame is a minor part of a worker's footprint; what disk-backing
removes is the parent's copy of the whole cohort.

The store is a plain directory you can inspect, copy and archive:
\preformatted{
cohort.mif/
  manifest.json      format, ids, and per-sample rows/columns/size
  clinical.rds  sample.rds
  spatial/  <sample>.parquet    one file per sample, never rewritten
  overlay/  <sample>.parquet    columns added later by split_tissue()
  derived/  <slot>.rds
}
Paths inside \code{manifest.json} are relative, so the whole directory can be moved
or copied and still open.
}
\section{What is and is not preserved}{

Column names, column order, types and factor levels -- including unused levels
and \code{NA}s -- round-trip exactly, so \code{collect_mif(mif_to_disk(x, p))} returns the
spatial data \code{x} had.

The spatial slot is iterable, so \code{sapply(mif$spatial, nrow)} and
\code{lapply(mif$spatial, ...)} work the same way they do on an in-memory \code{mif}. \strong{But
iterating reads every sample}, because \code{lapply()} materialises its input before
applying anything, so peak memory becomes the whole cohort — the cost
disk-backing exists to avoid. On a cohort that does not fit, iterate over names
instead and only one sample is resident at a time:

\preformatted{
# reads everything at once
sapply(mif$spatial, nrow)

# reads one sample at a time
vapply(names(mif$spatial), function(s) nrow(mif$spatial[[s]]), numeric(1))
}

Writing to the slot is refused outright — \code{mif$spatial[[i]] <- x} and
\code{length(mif$spatial) <- n} error rather than destroying the index. Use
\code{\link[=collect_mif]{collect_mif()}} to bring the data into memory if you need to modify it.

\strong{Row names are not preserved}; they come back as \code{1:nrow}. A columnar file has
nowhere to put them, and storing them would mean an extra column per sample for
something no function in this package reads -- every metric indexes cells by
position. Non-default row names only arise from having subset a data frame
(\code{spat[keep, ]}), and \code{dplyr} resets them anyway, so this is visible only if you
compare with \code{\link[=identical]{identical()}} after such a subset. Use
\code{all.equal(check.attributes = FALSE)}, or reset them yourself, if you need that
comparison to hold.
}

